From b8740baeff6d9806079cc2f983d5830361d67555 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 09:56:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] feat(chexc): annotate the obvious stdx flows (migration
 phase 3, leaf packages)

The six packages whose whole capability surface is one or two flows: stdx.compress
(TarGzip's file entry points, FSException), stdx.crypto.common (the PEM decoders, the
native-memory helpers, CryptoException), stdx.net.tls.common and the crypto kit accessor
(the "kit is not set" throw), stdx.actors (ActorFuture.get re-throws whatever the actor
threw), stdx.string_intern, stdx.unittest.data (the csv/tsv/json strategy builders open a
file), stdx.effect (an unhandled command is what the default implementation raises). Each
of those packages now reports no capability diagnostic at all.

Annotation propagates: a clause on a leaf makes its callers report the requirement they
always had, so stdx.crypto.keys, stdx.crypto.x509 and stdx.net.http each gained a handful
of diagnostics they will lose again when their own ring is annotated. Net across stdx:
1,386 -> 1,401.

Left alone deliberately: 'Iterator.next' implementations that infer 'ContentFormatException'
over a clause-free 'Iterator.next' (needs the std.core ruling), 'Resource.close' overrides
for the same reason, and the CryptoKit interface, whose members' clauses have to land
together with every implementation across the crypto packages.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
